{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"REGRESSION FOUND IN THIS TICKET'S OWN CHANGE, AND FIXED — new patchset on Gerrit 1130.\n\nWHAT BROKE: `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/mutate/test_outbound_create_binding.py::\ntest_create_one_populates_binding_store` FAILED with `assert None is not None`.\n\nHOW I FOUND IT: a full `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler` sweep run locally while waiting on CI —\n1 failed, 817 passed. It was NOT caught earlier because the change's own held-out module\n(`test_index_lag_duplicate_heldout.py`) passes, and the three in-flight Gerrit changes had been\nsitting in a saturated Actions queue for over two hours without reporting Verified.\n\nMECHANISM, traced rather than guessed. `create_one`'s new index-lag gate (dispatch_one.py:249-254)\nreads `getattr(binding_store, \"is_keyless_pending_within_grace\", None)` and defers the create when\nit returns truthy. That test's double is a bare `MagicMock()`, which answers EVERY member with a\ntruthy Mock — so the gate fired for a ticket that has no pending binding at all, the create was\ndeferred, and `create_one` returned None.\n\nI CONFIRMED IT WAS MINE RATHER THAN ASSUMING: reverted ONLY `dispatch_one.py` to HEAD~1 and re-ran —\nstill red, which ruled out my later 7c26 commit. Then `git log -S is_keyless_pending_within_grace`\nnamed commit 06b8702b1, this ticket's own change, as the introducer.\n\nTHE FIX: the test double now answers `is_keyless_pending_within_grace -> False`, which is what a\nREAL store returns for a ticket with no pending binding. This is a test-double fidelity fix, not an\noracle weakened to fit: the test's intent (\"create_one must call bind_confirm after a successful\ncreate\") is untouched and still asserted, and `bind_confirm.assert_called_once_with(...)` still\nruns. Without it the double stands for a store state that cannot occur. The reason is recorded in a\ncomment at the mock so the next reader does not delete it as noise.\n\nSCOPE CHECKED: this is the ONLY bare-MagicMock binding store in the test tree (grepped), so no\nsibling sites are affected. Re-ran with the fix: 16 passed in the two directly-affected modules,\nthen 581 passed across mutate/, diffing/, binding_walk and all three held-out modules in this stack.\n\nHOW IT LANDED IN THIS CHANGE RATHER THAN A LATER ONE: the fix belongs in the commit that introduced\nthe gate, or change 1130 goes green only when a LATER change happens to be applied — and Gerrit\nverifies each change independently, so 1130 alone would still have earned Verified -1. The stack was\nrebuilt non-interactively (amend 21fc, cherry-pick 387d and 7c26 back on top) with all three\nChange-Ids preserved, so this updated the existing changes rather than forking duplicates. The\nLLM-Review +1 on 1130 was correctly invalidated by the new code and needs a fresh round; 1131 and\n1136 were trivial rebases and kept their votes.\n\nLESSON, since this epic keeps recording them: a bare `MagicMock` is not a neutral stand-in for a\ncollaborator — every new `getattr`-guarded capability check silently changes what it answers, and\nthe failure surfaces as an unrelated-looking `None`. When adding a gate that consults an OPTIONAL\nstore member, grep the test tree for bare mocks of that collaborator in the same change."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785535286239416001,"uuid":"22947d87-1a6b-4c7b-9e29-4096a5da0f8a"}